  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14096769/assets/02ed30bbb45715a9c33f2c7dad79ce2dd17dd065d96fbd6fbb46a1b366456df8.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(9,1)$ and passes through the point $(-7,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(9,1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}=4p(x-9).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-7,5),$ we substitute $x=-7$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-1)^{2} & =4p(-7-9) \\\\ 16 & =4p(-16) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =-(x-9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =-(0-9) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-2,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $-2$ and $4.$",
